# The agent as an init service, for a device whose system image you control.
#
# Install to /system/etc/init/oarlock.rc, with the binary at /system/bin/oarlock-agent.
#
# Why this exists at all: on stock Android there is no way for an MDM to run a command, so
# reaching a device means an app, an app means a foreground service, and an app-sandbox
# session can see the app's own files and very little else. If the image is yours, an init
# service skips all of it — no app, no notification, no battery-optimisation exemption to
# beg for, and it comes back by itself when it dies.
#
# ── the only line that really matters ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   user shell
#
# init runs a service as root unless told otherwise, and the agent hands a session
# whatever identity it holds. Without this line every recorded shell on every machine is
# a root shell. uid 2000 (AID_SHELL) is what `adb shell` runs as: it can reach pm, am,
# dumpsys, logcat and settings, it cannot reach another app's data, and its SELinux domain
# has been hardened for a decade by people who do this full time. Start there and add
# only what a technician demonstrably needs.
#
# For two privilege tiers on one device — `exec` for field service, `shell` for on-call —
# drop `user shell` here, let init start the agent as root, and set `sessions:` in the
# config instead. The agent then drops per session and no session is ever root. That is
# the shape sshd has, and it is the only reason running this as root is defensible.

service oarlock /system/bin/oarlock-agent -config /data/vendor/oarlock/agent.conf
    class late_start
    user shell
    group shell log inet
    #                ^ inet is AID_INET (3003). Without it socket() fails and the agent
    #                  cannot dial the gateway at all. log (1007) is for logcat.
    seclabel u:r:shell:s0
    # SELinux, not the uid, is the real boundary on Android — even root is confined.
    # Reusing the shell domain inherits a policy somebody already got right; a dedicated
    # u:r:oarlock:s0 is better isolation and a week of chasing denials.

    # capabilities NET_RAW
    # ^ uncomment only if a technician genuinely needs tcpdump. A capability is a far
    #   smaller grant than root and should be reached for before `user root` ever is.

    restart_period 10
    # Ten seconds, not immediately: a gateway that is down should not be met with a
    # reconnect loop from the whole fleet at once. The agent has its own backoff for a
    # channel that drops; this is for a process that exits.

    oneshot false
    disabled false

# The device id, once, at first boot.
#
# init cannot reliably expand a property into a service argument, so the id is written to
# a file the agent reads (`device_file:`). Serial is the obvious choice; an asset id from
# your own provisioning is usually the better one.
on post-fs-data
    mkdir /data/vendor/oarlock 0750 shell shell
    exec -- /system/bin/sh -c "test -s /data/vendor/oarlock/device-id || getprop ro.serialno > /data/vendor/oarlock/device-id"
    chown shell shell /data/vendor/oarlock/device-id
    chmod 0640 /data/vendor/oarlock/device-id
